    </figure>
  </observation>

  <example>
    <title>A coordinate system on a plane</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Define
        <md>
<mrow>v_1 = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
0 \\
1
\end{pmatrix},\; v_2 = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
1 \\
1
\end{pmatrix},\quad
        \cB = \{v_1,\,v_2\}, \quad V = \Span\{v_1,\,v_2\}</mrow>
</md>.
        <ol>
          <li><p>Verify that <m>\cB</m> is a basis for <m>V</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>If <m>[w]_\cB = {5\choose 2}</m>, then what is <m>w</m>?</p></li>
          <li><p>Find the <m>\cB</m>-coordinates of
          <m>v = \begin{pmatrix}
5 \\
3 \\
5
\end{pmatrix}.</m>
          </p></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>
            We need to verify that <m>\cB</m> spans <m>V</m>, and that it is linearly independent.  By definition, <m>V</m> is the span of <m>\cB</m>; since <m>v_1</m> and <m>v_2</m> are not multiples of each other, they are linearly independent.  This shows in particular that <m>V</m> is a <em>plane</em>.
          </p></li>
          <li><p>
            To say <m>[w]_\cB={5\choose 2}</m> means that <m>5</m> is the <m>v_1</m>-coordinate of <m>w</m>, and that <m>2</m> is the <m>v_2</m>-coordinate:
            <md>
<mrow>w = 5v_1 + 2v_2 = 5\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
0 \\
1
\end{pmatrix} + 2\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
1 \\
1
\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
7 \\
2 \\
7
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          </p></li>
          <li><p>
            We have to solve the vector equation <m>v=c_1v_1 + c_2v_2</m> in the unknowns <m>c_1,c_2</m>.  We form an augmented matrix and row reduce:
            <md>
<mrow>\left(\begin{array}{rr|r}
1 \amp 1 \amp 5 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 3 \\
1 \amp 1 \amp 5
\end{array}\right)
            \quad\xrightarrow{\text{RREF}}\quad
            \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}
1 \amp 0 \amp 2 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 3 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 0
\end{array}\right)</mrow>
</md>.
            We have <m>c_1=2</m> and <m>c_2=3</m>, so <m>v = 2v_1+3v_2</m> and
            <m>[v]_\cB = {2\choose 3}.</m>
          </p></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="dimension-coordinates-1">
        <caption>A picture of the plane <m>V</m> and the basis <m>\cB = \{v_1,v_2\}</m>.  The violet grid is a picture of the coordinate system defined by the basis <m>\cB</m>; one set of lines measures the <m>v_1</m>-coordinate, and the other set measures the <m>v_2</m>-coordinate.  Use the sliders to find the <m>\cB</m>-coordinates of <m>v</m>.</caption>
        <interactive
            iframe="iframes/spans-if.html?v1=1,0,1&amp;v2=1,1,1&amp;target=5,3,5&amp;range=6&amp;camera=-3,0,2&amp;tlabel=v"
            width="90%"
            aspect="4:3">
          <shortdescription>
            <!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this interactive (linear algebra context) -->
          </shortdescription>
          <description>
            <!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this interactive for a linear algebra course -->
            <p>
              
            </p>
          </description>
          <instructions>
            <p>
              
            </p>
          </instructions>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example>
    <title>A coordinate system on another plane</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Define
      <md>
<mrow>v_1 = \begin{pmatrix}
2 \\
3 \\
2
\end{pmatrix},\; v_2 = \begin{pmatrix}
-1 \\
1 \\
1
\end{pmatrix},\; v_3 = \begin{pmatrix}
2 \\
8 \\
6
\end{pmatrix},\quad
      V = \Span\{v_1,\,v_2,\,v_3\}</mrow>
</md>.
      <ol>
        <li><p>Find a basis <m>\cB</m> for <m>V</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p>Find the <m>\cB</m>-coordinates of <m>x = \begin{pmatrix}
4 \\
11 \\
8
\end{pmatrix}</m>.</p></li>
      </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>
            We write <m>V</m> as the column space of a matrix <m>A</m>, then row reduce to find the pivot columns, as in <xref ref="dimension-eg-basis-span"/>.
            <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp -1 \amp 2 \\
3 \amp 1 \amp 8 \\
2 \amp 1 \amp 6
\end{pmatrix} \quad\xrightarrow{\text{RREF}}\quad
            \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp 2 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
            The first two columns are pivot columns, so we can take <m>\cB=\{v_1,v_2\}</m> as our basis for <m>V</m>.
          </p></li>
          <li><p>
            We have to solve the vector equation <m>x = c_1v_1 + c_2v_2</m>.  We form an augmented matrix and row reduce:
            <md>
<mrow>\left(\begin{array}{rr|r}
2 \amp -1 \amp 4 \\
3 \amp 1 \amp 11 \\
2 \amp 1 \amp 8
\end{array}\right)
            \quad\xrightarrow{\text{RREF}}\quad
            \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}
1 \amp 0 \amp 3 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 0
\end{array}\right)</mrow>
</md>.
            We have <m>c_1 = 3</m> and <m>c_2 = 2</m>, so
            <m>x=3v_1 + 2v_2</m>, and thus <m>[x]_\cB = {3\choose 2}.</m>
          </p></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="dimension-coordinates-2">
        <caption>A picture of the plane <m>V</m> and the basis <m>\cB = \{v_1,v_2\}</m>.  The violet grid is a picture of the coordinate system defined by the basis <m>\cB</m>; one set of lines measures the <m>v_1</m>-coordinate, and the other set measures the <m>v_2</m>-coordinate.  Use the sliders to find the <m>\cB</m>-coordinates of <m>x</m>.</caption>
        <interactive
            iframe="iframes/spans-if.html?v1=2,3,2&amp;v2=-1,1,1&amp;target=4,11,8&amp;tlabel=x&amp;range=12&amp;camera=.5,-3,2&amp;tlabel=x"
            width="90%"
